        ("Siri", "The Siri interface process. It draws the Siri window or menu bar orb and carries the request you speak or type over to the system's speech recognition and intent services; it stays loaded so invoking Siri feels instant."),
        ("Siri AI", "The newer Apple Intelligence-backed Siri app. It handles requests routed to the large language model side of Siri, using the on-device models where it can and Apple's Private Cloud Compute servers for harder ones."),
        ("SoftwareUpdateNotificationManager", "A helper app inside Apple's SoftwareUpdate framework. Its entire job is posting the alerts that tell you an update is available, downloaded, or about to install, and handling the buttons on those notifications."),
        ("SoftwareUpdateSettingsExtensionIntents", "An App Intents extension for the Software Update pane of System Settings. It hands the system that pane's settings and search terms so update options can be found through Spotlight or reached by Siri and Shortcuts."),
        ("Spokenly", "A third-party dictation app. It captures microphone audio and turns your speech into text you can type into any app, which is why it stays running in the background waiting for its hotkey."),
        ("SpotlightSettingsIntentsExtension", "An App Intents extension covering the Spotlight pane in System Settings. It exports the search categories and privacy options found there so they are themselves searchable and can be reached from Siri and Shortcuts."),
        ("StartupDiskWidgetExtension", "A WidgetKit extension belonging to the Startup Disk settings. It draws the small widget describing the disk your Mac boots from, waking briefly on the widget refresh schedule and then exiting."),
        ("StatusKitAgent", "The agent for StatusKit, Apple's framework for live status displays such as Live Activities. It keeps those small ongoing status items updated, including ones started on another device and mirrored to your Mac."),
        ("StocksWidget", "The WidgetKit extension from the Stocks app. It wakes periodically to fetch quotes and redraw your stocks widget on the desktop or in Notification Center, so prices stay current without Stocks being open."),
        ("StorageSettingsIntentsExtension", "An App Intents extension for the Storage pane of System Settings. It publishes that pane's entries so a search for something like disk space finds it, and so Shortcuts and Siri can reference those settings."),
        ("SubmitDiagInfo", "The system service that uploads diagnostic and crash reports to Apple. It runs briefly after a crash or on a schedule to hand off the logs the system collected, and only sends anything if you agreed to share analytics."),
        ("T3 Code (Nightly)", "The nightly build of the T3 Code editor, built on Electron. Because Electron bundles a Chromium browser and a Node runtime, the main app spawns a family of helper processes alongside its windows."),
        ("T3 Code (Nightly) Helper", "A generic Electron helper process spawned by T3 Code. Chromium splits its work across processes, and these helpers handle supporting jobs like networking, plugins, and GPU work, so several can exist under similar names at once."),
        ("T3 Code (Nightly) Helper (Renderer)", "The Electron renderer process for a T3 Code window or tab. It runs the editor interface and its JavaScript, sandboxed away from the main process; heavy CPU here usually means one busy window or extension."),
        ("TC3Q7MAJXF.com.adguard.safari.AdGuard.Helper", "A helper process for the AdGuard Safari extension, named after the developer's team ID and bundle identifier. It maintains and compiles the filter lists Safari uses to block ads and trackers, work too heavy to do inside the extension itself."),
        ("TGOnDeviceInferenceProviderService", "A system extension that provides on-device model inference for Apple Intelligence features. It loads and runs the local models in its own sandboxed process, so requests handled without the cloud show up as CPU or Neural Engine time here."),
        ("Tailscale", "The Tailscale client for its mesh VPN. It keeps an authenticated connection to Tailscale's coordination servers and maintains encrypted peer links to your other machines, so those devices stay reachable by name from anywhere."),
        ("TeamsWidgetExtension", "The WidgetKit extension bundled with Microsoft Teams. It periodically renders the Teams widget with things like upcoming meetings or recent activity, and can run in the background even when Teams itself is closed."),
        ("TextSniper", "A third-party OCR utility. Its shortcut lets you drag a selection over anything on screen, then recognizes the text inside and puts it on the clipboard, which is how you copy from images, scanned PDFs, and video."),
        ("TimeMachineSettingsIntents", "An App Intents extension covering Time Machine's settings. It makes backup options discoverable through System Settings search and Spotlight and reachable from Siri and Shortcuts; it launches only when something queries it."),
        ("TipsWidgetExtension", "The WidgetKit extension inside the Tips app. It renders the widget that surfaces short macOS how-to suggestions, waking on the widget refresh schedule and exiting again."),
        ("ToolStatusDashboard", "A locally built SwiftUI app rather than an Apple component. It runs health checks over installed command line tools and scheduled jobs and shows each result as a pass or fail card, so broken tooling surfaces instead of failing quietly."),
        ("UIKitSystem", "A system app that hosts the UIKit environment on macOS. iPhone and iPad apps running on Apple silicon expect iOS-style system services, and this process supplies them; it appears once such an app is launched."),
        ("UVCAssistant", "A userspace driver process inside the CoreMediaIO framework, packaged as a system extension. It implements the USB Video Class protocol, so any plug-in webcam that works without a vendor driver is actually being run by this process."),
        ("UniversalControl", "The system process behind Universal Control. It handles carrying one keyboard and pointer between your Mac and a nearby Mac or iPad, noticing when the cursor reaches a screen edge and forwarding your input to the other device."),
        ("Unsplash Wallpapers", "A third-party app that sets your desktop picture from Unsplash's photo library. It runs in the background to download new images and rotate the wallpaper on whatever schedule you configured."),
        ("UsageQueue", "A user-installed menu bar utility of Ivo's own making, not an Apple component. It holds a message you have written and automatically delivers it into an existing Claude or Codex session once your usage window resets, so a queued prompt runs the moment quota is available again."),
        ("UserNotificationCenter", "The user-facing agent behind the Notification Center UI. It draws the banners, alerts, and grouped notification stacks that apps request through the notification frameworks, and it stays resident so a notification can appear even when the app that posted it is not frontmost."),
        ("ViewBridgeAuxiliary", "A helper process for AppKit's ViewBridge mechanism, which lets one app display UI that is actually drawn and owned by another process. It is how a sandboxed app can show a system panel such as Open, Save, or Share without ever gaining access to the files behind it."),
        ("WallpaperAerialsExtension", "An extension of the Wallpaper framework that renders Apple's Aerial video screen savers and the matching dynamic desktop pictures. It runs when an aerial is playing or transitioning into a still wallpaper, and it is the process that decodes those large video files."),
        ("WallpaperAgent", "The per-user daemon that owns desktop picture state. It tracks which wallpaper each display and Space is using, applies light and dark or time-of-day variants, and hands the current image to the window server; it briefly wakes whenever you change wallpaper or connect a display."),
        ("WallpaperImageExtension", "A Wallpaper framework extension that handles ordinary still-image desktop pictures. It loads, decodes, and scales the photo or system image you picked to fit each display, which is the cheap counterpart to the aerial and dynamic wallpaper extensions."),
        ("WiFiAgent", "The user-level agent of the Wi-Fi stack, sitting above the CoreWLAN daemon. It drives the Wi-Fi menu bar item and any connection dialogs, so it is what populates the network list, prompts you for a password, and shows the captive portal sign-in sheet on hotel or airport networks."),
        ("WiFiMacSettingsIntents", "An App Intents extension bundled with the Wi-Fi settings components. It exposes Wi-Fi actions and settings to the system-wide intents layer, which is what lets Spotlight, Siri, and Shortcuts find and run things like toggling Wi-Fi without opening System Settings."),
        ("WindowManager", "The daemon behind Stage Manager and related window arrangement features. It tracks which windows belong to which app grouping, animates them in and out of the side strip, and handles the tiling and window layout behavior added alongside Stage Manager."),
        ("WirelessRadioManagerd", "A coordinating daemon for the Mac's wireless radios. Rather than driving any single radio itself, it arbitrates between Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, and cellular-style state so that things like Airplane Mode, radio power policy, and coexistence between radios sharing the same frequencies behave consistently."),
        ("WordWidget_mac", "A widget extension bundled inside Microsoft Word, living in the app's PlugIns folder. It supplies the Word widget you can place in Notification Center or on the desktop, typically showing recent documents; it runs only while that widget is visible or refreshing."),
        ("WorldClockWidget", "The WidgetKit extension that renders the World Clock widget from Apple's Clock app. It is launched briefly by the widget daemon to redraw the clock faces for the cities you added, then exits; it is not the process that keeps your system time correct."),
        ("XProtectBridgeService", "An XPC service inside Apple's XprotectFramework. It acts as the go-between that hands current malware signatures and policy data to the scanning code, so the part of the system that inspects files is kept separate from the part that manages the definitions."),
        ("XprotectService", "The scanning half of XProtect, Apple's built-in anti-malware system. It checks apps and downloaded files against Apple's known-malware signatures at launch and during periodic sweeps; you normally never see it unless it blocks something and shows a malware warning."),
        ("adid", "A small daemon from Apple's private CoreADI framework, which deals with device identity and attestation data used when this Mac authenticates to Apple's services. It reacts to changes in the machine's identity, so it is more about proving the device is what it claims than about advertising."),
        ("agy", "A local command-line agent binary in Ivo's own ~/.local/bin, not part of macOS. It runs prompt sessions and powers the personal memory search and indexing tooling on this Mac, which is why it appears during memory queries, nightly indexing jobs, and other local automation."),
        ("amsaccountsd", "A daemon in the Apple Media Services stack, the shared plumbing behind the App Store, Music, TV, and Books. It keeps track of which Apple Account is signed in for media purchases and handles the authentication those storefronts need before they can show your library or let you buy."),
        ("amsondevicestoraged", "An Apple Media Services daemon built on the OnDeviceStorage framework. It keeps a running tally of how much space downloaded media and app content occupies and broadcasts changes to it, which is what feeds the per-category storage numbers you see in System Settings."),
        ("analyticsagent", "The user-side agent of Apple's diagnostics and usage reporting system. It gathers crash logs and analytics data and, only if you opted in to sharing with Apple or developers, queues them for upload; if you declined sharing it mostly sits idle."),
        ("aned", "The system daemon for the Apple Neural Engine, the dedicated machine-learning block on Apple silicon. It manages access to that hardware and schedules model execution on it, so it wakes for things like Face detection in Photos, dictation, and other on-device ML features."),
        ("aneuserd", "The user-space companion to the Neural Engine daemon. It brokers requests from ordinary apps down to the Neural Engine hardware, keeping each app's model workloads separated and running outside the kernel; it is active whenever an app runs a Core ML model on the ANE."),
        ("apfsd", "A maintenance daemon for APFS, the filesystem macOS stores everything on. It handles background housekeeping such as reclaiming space from deleted snapshots and container-level upkeep, work that is spread out over idle time so it never blocks what you are doing."),
        ("app.spokenly.autolaunch", "A tiny login helper bundled inside the third-party Spokenly app. Its only job is to start Spokenly when you log in; it is registered as a login item, which is why it shows up under Login Items and Background Items in System Settings."),
        ("appleaccountd", "The daemon that manages your Apple Account on this Mac. It stores the signed-in identity, refreshes authentication tokens, and answers other services that need to know who you are, which is why an expired token surfaces as a repeated Apple Account password prompt."),
        ("appleeventsd", "The system daemon that routes Apple Events, the decades-old messaging system that lets one app tell another to do something. It is what carries AppleScript commands, Automator actions, and inter-app requests, and it is the component that shows the prompt asking whether one app may control another."),
        ("assessmentagent", "The agent for Automatic Assessment Configuration, the framework schools use for locked-down exams. When a testing app starts an assessment session it puts the Mac into a restricted mode; despite the name it is unrelated to Gatekeeper's checks on whether an app is safe to open."),
        ("assetsubscriptiond", "A daemon that subscribes to and downloads large on-device model and data assets from Apple. It is the mechanism that fetches Apple Intelligence models and similar bundles in the background, which is why it can pull significant amounts of data after a major system update."),
        ("audioaccessoryd", "A daemon that manages paired audio accessories such as AirPods and Beats headphones. It tracks their connection state, battery levels, and firmware, and handles the automatic switching that moves audio to your headphones when you put them in."),
        ("autofsd", "A small daemon that is part of the automount system. It watches for the conditions that require a network share to be mounted on demand and triggers the actual mounting machinery, so paths under /net and similar automount maps appear only when something touches them."),
        ("automountd", "The daemon that performs on-demand mounting of network filesystems such as NFS and SMB. When a process reaches into an automounted path it does the mount right then and unmounts it again after a period of disuse, which keeps unused shares from holding connections open."),
        ("axassetsd", "A daemon built on the AXAssetLoader framework that fetches Accessibility resources on demand. It downloads and updates things like the high-quality VoiceOver voices and other feature data, so it is usually busy right after you enable an accessibility feature and idle otherwise."),
        ("backgroundtaskmanagementd", "The daemon behind background task scheduling and the Login Items and Extensions list. It decides when deferred background work is allowed to run based on power and thermal conditions, and it tracks which apps have installed background items so macOS can tell you when a new one appears."),
        ("bash", "An instance of the Bourne-Again shell running a script or an interactive session. On its own it means very little; what matters is the command it was started to run, which may be a Terminal session, a scheduled job, or a build step launched by another app."),
        ("biomed", "The daemon for Biome, Apple's on-device stream of behavioral events such as app launches, locations, and interactions. Other features read those streams to make suggestions, so it is part of the machinery behind Siri Suggestions and proactive recommendations rather than a sync service itself."),
        ("biomesyncd", "The syncing counterpart to the Biome daemon. It reconciles those on-device event streams between your Mac, iPhone, and iPad through your Apple Account, which is how suggestions and Continuity-style handoffs stay consistent no matter which device you were using."),
        ("biometrickitd", "The daemon backing BiometricKit, the framework for Touch ID. It talks to the Secure Enclave, which is where fingerprint data actually lives and is matched, and it relays only a yes-or-no answer, which is what unlocks your Mac or approves an Apple Pay purchase."),
        ("boringNotch", "A third-party app that draws its own interface in and around the MacBook's camera notch, in the style of the iPhone's Dynamic Island. It shows now-playing controls, charging status, and similar glanceable items, and stays resident because it must react to those events."),
        ("browser_crashpad_handler", "The Crashpad handler process used by Chromium-based apps such as Chrome, Electron apps, and VS Code. It sits idle alongside the browser watching for a crash in any of its processes, then captures a minidump; one such process per running Chromium app is normal."),
        ("bun.exe", "The Bun JavaScript runtime, here shipped inside an npm package's node_modules rather than installed system-wide. Despite the .exe suffix this is the macOS binary; it is running because some JavaScript tool on this machine chose Bun instead of Node to execute its code."),
        ("businessservicesd", "A daemon from Apple's BusinessChatService framework, the plumbing for Messages for Business. It handles brand information and the connection to companies that offer support chat through Messages, so it is relevant only when you contact a business that way."),
        ("caffeinate", "A small command-line utility that asserts a power management lock to prevent sleep. Something started it deliberately, either you from Terminal or a script wrapping a long job, and the Mac will not idle-sleep or dim as long as it is running."),
        ("calaccessd", "A daemon in the CalendarDaemon framework that mediates access to your calendar database. Apps do not read the calendar store directly; they ask this process, which enforces the per-app Calendar privacy permission you granted or denied in System Settings."),
        ("chronod", "The daemon behind WidgetKit, internally named Chrono. It manages every widget on your desktop and in Notification Center, deciding when each one's timeline should refresh and launching the owning app's widget extension to redraw it; it is not related to Screen Time."),
        ("claude", "The Claude Code command-line tool from Anthropic, a user-installed developer program rather than part of macOS. It runs an AI coding agent in the terminal that can read and edit files and run commands, so it is typically active only while you have a session open."),
        ("clocksyncd", "A daemon in Apple's TimeSync stack that keeps hardware clocks aligned. It works with the IOTimeSync kernel services to synchronize timing across connected hardware and paired devices, precision that matters for things like audio and video gear rather than for the wall clock in your menu bar."),
        ("codex", "The OpenAI Codex command-line tool, a user-installed developer program. Like other terminal agents it reads your project, proposes and applies edits, and runs commands on request; it is running because a Codex session or a scripted Codex job was started."),
        ("codex-code-mode-host", "A helper binary in Ivo's ~/.local/bin that hosts Codex's code mode. It runs the sandboxed sessions in which the agent executes code, keeping each request in its own isolated cell so that generated code runs apart from the main CLI process."),
        ("com.apple.AppleUserHIDDrivers", "A DriverKit extension hosting Apple's human interface device drivers in user space rather than in the kernel. It handles the built-in keyboard and trackpad plus attached mice and other HID gear, so a crash here degrades input instead of panicking the whole machine."),
        ("com.apple.AuthenticationServices.Helper", "A helper process for the AuthenticationServices framework. It presents the system sheets for Sign in with Apple, passkeys, and password AutoFill, and it is what lets a website or app collect a credential without the app itself ever seeing your keychain."),
        ("com.apple.CloudDocs.iCloudDriveFileProvider", "The File Provider extension that presents iCloud Drive in Finder. It makes remote files look like local ones, downloads a file's contents the moment you open it, and evicts rarely used files when Optimize Mac Storage is on."),
        ("com.apple.CodeSigningHelper", "An XPC service inside the Security framework that performs code signature evaluation out of process. Other components ask it whether a binary's signature is valid and who signed it, which is a building block under Gatekeeper, notarization checks, and app entitlement enforcement."),
        ("com.apple.CoreSimulator.CoreSimulatorService", "The background service that manages Xcode's simulators. It creates, boots, and shuts down simulated iOS, watchOS, and tvOS devices and keeps their state on disk; it stays running after you close Xcode and is the usual culprit when a simulator gets stuck."),
        ("com.apple.DriverKit-AppleBCMWLAN", "A DriverKit extension that runs the Broadcom Wi-Fi chip's driver in user space. It handles the low-level work of talking to the wireless hardware, sitting underneath the Wi-Fi daemons and the menu bar UI you actually interact with."),
        ("com.apple.DriverKit-IOUserDockChannelSerial", "A user-space DriverKit extension for the dock channel serial interface on Apple silicon. It exposes a low-level serial link over the internal hardware channel, an internal plumbing detail with no user-visible feature attached to it."),
        ("com.apple.Notes.SpotlightIndexExtension", "A Spotlight indexing extension shipped inside the Notes app. It reads note contents and hands searchable text to the Spotlight index, which is why typing a phrase from a note into Spotlight finds it; it wakes when notes change and exits again."),
        ("com.apple.Notes.WidgetExtension", "The WidgetKit extension inside the Notes app. It draws the Notes widget on your desktop or in Notification Center, and the widget daemon launches it briefly whenever that widget needs a fresh snapshot rather than keeping it running all the time."),
        ("com.apple.Passwords.MenuBarExtra", "The menu bar component of Apple's Passwords app. It provides the quick menu for searching and copying saved passwords and verification codes without opening the full app, and it runs only if you enabled that menu bar item."),
        ("com.apple.Safari.History", "A Safari XPC service that owns the browsing history database. Keeping history in its own process means web pages and the browser UI go through it rather than touching the database directly; it also handles syncing history to your other devices via iCloud."),
        ("com.apple.Safari.SafeBrowsing.Service", "A Safari XPC service implementing fraudulent site warnings. It checks a privacy-preserving fingerprint of the site you are visiting against a list of known phishing and malware sites, and it is what produces the full-page red warning before a dangerous page loads."),
        ("com.apple.Safari.SearchHelper", "A Safari XPC service that handles search-field behavior. It fetches suggestions from your chosen search engine and merges them with Safari's own top hits and history matches, which is the dropdown that appears as you type in the address bar."),
        ("com.apple.SafariPlatformSupport.Helper", "A helper process for Safari's platform support component, which supplies shared browser configuration and support data. It runs on Safari's behalf to keep that supporting work out of the main browser process; it has no interface of its own."),
        ("com.apple.WebKit.WebContent.EnhancedSecurity", "A WebKit web content process running in a hardened configuration. It renders a page's HTML and JavaScript with extra mitigations and reduced attack surface, used for Lockdown Mode and other cases where safety matters more than the fastest possible rendering."),
        ("com.apple.accessibility.mediaaccessibilityd", "The daemon behind the MediaAccessibility framework. It holds your preferences for closed captions and subtitles, including the custom caption styling and the setting to prefer SDH or audio descriptions, and supplies them to any app playing video."),
        ("com.apple.appkit.xpc.openAndSavePanelService", "The XPC service that actually implements AppKit's Open and Save dialogs. Because it runs outside the app, a sandboxed app can show you a full file browser yet only receive access to the one file you pick; the panel you see belongs to this process, not the app."),
        ("com.apple.audio.SandboxHelper", "A helper that hosts third-party audio plug-ins outside of the app using them. Running an Audio Unit in this separate sandboxed process means a badly behaved plug-in can crash without taking your audio app down with it, and it limits what the plug-in can reach."),
        ("com.apple.cmio.registerassistantservice", "A service in the CoreMedia I/O stack, the layer that handles cameras and other video capture devices. It registers capture plug-ins, including virtual cameras from apps like conferencing and streaming tools, so that other apps can see them in their camera list."),
        ("com.apple.cmio.videodriverkithostextension", "A host process for user-space camera drivers written against DriverKit and CoreMedia I/O. It runs the driver for a capture device outside the kernel, which is how a webcam or virtual camera can be supported without kernel extensions."),
        ("com.apple.dock.external.extra.arm64", "An XPC service bundled inside Dock.app that handles Dock instances on additional displays. It exists so a second Dock can be drawn on an external monitor separately from the main one; it appears only when an external display is attached."),
        ("com.apple.geod", "An XPC service inside the GeoServices framework, the private stack behind Apple Maps. It does the network side of location work for any app that asks: turning coordinates into street addresses and back, and fetching map tiles and place data."),
        ("com.apple.hiservices-xpcservice", "An XPC helper for HIServices, the Human Interface Services part of ApplicationServices. It does out-of-process work for accessibility and window-level services, so it typically appears when VoiceOver, Zoom, or an app driving another app through the accessibility API is active."),
        ("com.apple.iCloudHelper", "An XPC service inside AOSKit, the framework that handles Apple Account plumbing. It runs iCloud account operations out of process for System Settings and other apps: validating credentials, checking account state, and fetching the settings your iCloud services depend on."),
        ("com.apple.mail.SpotlightIndexExtension", "A Spotlight indexing app-extension bundled inside Mail.app. macOS launches it in the background to read your messages and hand their text to the Spotlight index, which is why searching a sender or a phrase finds mail without opening Mail."),
        ("com.apple.podcasts.SpotlightIndexExtension", "A Spotlight indexing app-extension bundled inside Podcasts.app. It feeds show and episode information into the Spotlight index so podcasts turn up in Spotlight and Siri results; it runs briefly after your library changes."),
        ("com.crystalidea.macsfancontrol.smcwrite", "A privileged root helper installed by the third-party app Macs Fan Control, living in /Library/PrivilegedHelperTools. Writing to the SMC, the chip that governs fans, requires root, so the app talks to this small helper instead of running as root itself."),
        ("com.ivogundlach.vitals.helper", "The optional privileged helper for Vitals itself, installed as a launchd daemon so it can read data the app cannot reach as a normal user process. It only runs if you opted in, and Vitals talks to it over XPC for things like full per-process detail."),
        ("commerce", "A daemon in the CommerceKit framework, the private stack shared by the App Store and in-app purchases. It handles purchase authorization, receipts, and download entitlements, so it wakes when you buy or redownload an app and stays idle otherwise."),
        ("contactsd", "The background daemon for the Contacts database. It keeps your address book in sync with iCloud, Google, and Exchange accounts, and it also links duplicate cards together so Mail, Messages, and Phone can show one name instead of three."),
        ("containermanagerd", "The system daemon that owns app sandbox containers, the private ~/Library/Containers folders each sandboxed app is confined to. It creates them, sets their permissions, and cleans them up when an app is removed; this is the per-user instance."),
        ("containermanagerd_system", "The system-wide counterpart to containermanagerd, running as root outside any user session. It manages sandbox container directories for system daemons and for apps at the machine level rather than for a logged-in user."),
        ("coreauthd", "The daemon behind LocalAuthentication, the framework apps use to ask for identity checks. It runs the Touch ID and password prompts you see when unlocking a keychain item, approving a System Settings change, or paying with Apple Pay, and it is what keeps apps from touching the biometric data directly."),
        ("corespeechd", "The per-user daemon of the CoreSpeech framework, which does on-device speech processing. It handles the audio side of Dictation and the Hey Siri trigger phrase, so it becomes active when you talk to your Mac and idles the rest of the time."),
        ("corespeechd_system", "The system-level instance of the CoreSpeech daemon, running outside any user session. It covers speech work that has to happen before or independent of a login, such as listening for the Siri trigger phrase at the login window."),
        ("countryd", "A small system daemon that works out which country or region the Mac is currently in, using signals such as network and location data. Regional rules ride on its answer, including which features are legally available and which regional defaults apply."),
        ("cron", "The classic Unix job scheduler, inherited from BSD and still present alongside launchd. It runs commands from crontab files at fixed times, so it is active if you or an installed tool created a crontab; on a stock Mac it usually has nothing to do."),
        ("ctkahp", "A CryptoTokenKit bundle called Smartcard Agent, part of the framework that talks to smart cards and security keys. It supports card-backed authentication, the flow where inserting a card or tapping a key satisfies a login or unlock prompt."),
        ("ctkd", "The CryptoTokenKit daemon, the middle layer between the security system and hardware tokens. It loads smart card drivers and keeps card sessions alive, so the certificates and keys on a PIV card or a YubiKey appear to apps as ordinary keychain items."),
        ("cursor-agent", "The command-line agent from the Cursor editor, a third-party AI coding tool, installed in your ~/.local/bin. It is running because you or a script started it; it is not part of macOS and can be quit safely."),
        ("dataaccessd", "The account sync engine for calendar, contacts, and mail accounts that use open protocols. It is what actually talks CalDAV, CardDAV, and Exchange to Google, iCloud, and work servers, which is why a new event on the web shows up on your Mac without you refreshing anything."),
        ("devicecheckd", "The client daemon for DeviceCheck, Apple's app-attestation service. It lets an app's server confirm that a request really came from a genuine Apple device running a genuine copy of that app, which is how developers block fraud and free-trial abuse without tracking you."),
        ("diagnostics_agent", "A per-user agent in the diagnostics and reporting stack. It gathers crash and diagnostic reports on your Mac and manages sending them to Apple if you have analytics sharing turned on; it usually appears right after something crashes."),
        ("dirhelper", "A tiny privileged launchd helper whose only job is creating directories a user cannot create alone, most notably the per-user temporary folders behind TMPDIR. It is launched on demand and exits after a period of inactivity."),
        ("donotdisturbd", "The daemon behind Focus, the successor to Do Not Disturb. It holds your current Focus mode and its rules, decides which notifications get through or get held, and syncs the active Focus to your other Apple devices."),
        ("eligibilityd", "A system daemon that evaluates which Apple features and offers this Mac qualifies for, based on things like region, account, and hardware. Its answers gate whether certain services appear at all, which is why a feature can be present in the OS but hidden on your machine."),
        ("fairplayd", "The daemon for FairPlay, Apple's digital rights management system. It manages the decryption keys for protected content, so it is what lets purchased or rented movies, Apple TV episodes, and Apple Music tracks play back on this Mac and nowhere else."),
        ("feedbackd", "The daemon that drives haptic feedback on the Force Touch trackpad. It produces the taps you feel from the Taptic Engine when you force-click a word, drag a file into a snap position, or hit the end of a slider."),
        ("filecoordinationd", "The system arbiter for NSFileCoordinator, the API apps use to touch a file safely. When two processes want the same document, it serializes them and notifies the loser to reload, which is what keeps a file open in an app from being corrupted by a sync client rewriting it underneath."),
        ("fileproviderd", "The daemon behind the File Provider framework, the mechanism cloud storage uses to appear in Finder. It manages iCloud Drive plus third-party providers like Dropbox and Google Drive, handling on-demand downloads so a folder can look full while most of it still lives in the cloud."),
        ("financed", "A daemon in the Wallet and Apple Pay stack. It handles the financial account side of things: card and pass state, transaction data shown in Wallet, and features tied to Apple Card and Apple Cash where those are available."),
        ("findmybeaconingd", "The Find My beaconing daemon. It emits the encrypted Bluetooth broadcasts that let other people's Apple devices spot your Mac and anonymously report its location, which is how a lost Mac can still be found after it goes offline."),
        ("findmydevice-user-agent", "The per-user agent of Find My on this Mac. It keeps the device registered with your Apple Account, reports location when Find My asks, and carries out remote commands such as Play Sound, Lock, or Erase."),
        ("fontd", "The font management daemon inside ATS, the Apple Type Services part of ApplicationServices. It maintains the list of fonts installed for you and the system, activates and deactivates them, and answers apps that ask which fonts exist, which is what populates every font menu."),
        ("fontworker", "A worker process spawned by fontd to do the heavy or risky parts of font handling. It parses, validates, and caches font files off the main daemon, so a malformed font slows down or crashes the worker instead of the font system as a whole."),
        ("fskit_agent", "The per-user agent for FSKit, Apple's framework for filesystem drivers that run in userspace instead of the kernel. It reports which third-party filesystem modules you have available and launches them when fskitd asks."),
        ("fskitd", "The system daemon that manages FSKit mounts, the userspace filesystem drivers macOS now prefers over kernel extensions. It handles the mount lifecycle and translates kernel requests to the userspace driver, so it appears when you mount a volume in a format handled by a third-party driver."),
        ("gamecontrolleragentd", "The agent behind the Game Controller framework. It discovers and pairs Xbox, PlayStation, and MFi controllers over Bluetooth, applies your button remappings, and presents them to games in one uniform shape regardless of brand."),
        ("gamed", "The Game Center daemon. It handles your Game Center account, achievements, leaderboards, and multiplayer matchmaking, so it only has real work when a game that uses Game Center is running or signing in."),
        ("gamepolicyd", "The daemon behind Game Mode, despite the Game Center sounding name. It detects when a full-screen game is in front and coordinates the system policy change that gives it priority on CPU and GPU and doubles the Bluetooth sampling rate for controllers and AirPods."),
        ("generativeexperiencesd", "The runtime daemon for Apple Intelligence's generative features. It is the process behind Writing Tools, Image Playground, and similar generation requests, dispatching them to the on-device models and returning the result to the app that asked."),
        ("hybridsearchd", "A search daemon built on Apple's HybridSearch and Proactive frameworks. It builds and queries an on-device index that blends plain keyword matching with meaning-based matching, feeding the results Spotlight and Siri Suggestions show you."),
        ("imklaunchagent", "A small agent in InputMethodKit, the framework third-party input methods are written against. It launches and manages input method servers, so it appears once you use a non-Roman keyboard input source such as Pinyin, Kotoeri, or a third-party IME."),
        ("inputanalyticsd", "A daemon that collects aggregate statistics about keyboard and trackpad use. The data feeds improvements to things like autocorrect and gesture handling, and it only leaves the Mac if you have opted into sharing analytics with Apple."),
        ("intelligencecontextd", "A daemon in Apple Intelligence's context runtime. Its job is to assemble what is currently on screen and in the foreground app into context a model request can use, which is what lets you ask about the thing you are looking at without describing it."),
        ("intelligenceflowd", "The coordinator daemon for Apple Intelligence request flows. It sequences the steps of a single request, calling out to context, model, and tool components in order and assembling the result, so it is active for the duration of any Apple Intelligence interaction."),
        ("intelligencetasksd", "The scheduler for Apple Intelligence background work. It queues tasks that do not need to happen right now and runs them when the Mac is idle, plugged in, and cool, which is why this kind of processing tends to show up overnight."),
        ("intelligentroutingd", "The routing daemon for Apple Intelligence requests. It decides whether a given request can be answered by the models on your Mac or has to go out to Private Cloud Compute, weighing the size of the request against local capability."),
        ("kernelmanagerd", "The daemon that manages kernel extensions and DriverKit system extensions. It stages, validates, and loads them, and it is the process behind the approval prompt and restart you get when installing a driver from outside Apple."),
        ("launchservicesd", "The daemon behind Launch Services, the database mapping file types and URL schemes to applications. It decides which app opens a double-clicked file, populates the Open With menu, and tracks which apps are installed and running."),
        ("liveactivitiesd", "A daemon in the SessionCore framework that manages Live Activities, the compact ongoing updates for things like a delivery or a timer. On a Mac these usually arrive from a nearby iPhone and appear in the menu bar or Notification Center."),
        ("lockoutagent", "The agent that enforces lockout after repeated failed unlock attempts. It applies the escalating delay and shows the try again later message at the login window, and on Macs with Activation Lock it presents the recovery path."),
        ("logd_helper", "A helper process for logd, the unified logging daemon. It handles privileged or bulk log work on logd's behalf, such as collecting and archiving log data, which is why it shows up when a sysdiagnose or a log collect runs."),
        ("mDNSResponderHelper", "The privileged sidekick to mDNSResponder, the Bonjour daemon. mDNSResponder itself runs unprivileged for safety, so it hands the few operations needing root to this helper; together they are how your Mac finds printers, AirPlay targets, and .local names on the network."),
        ("maild", "A daemon in the EmailDaemon framework that does Mail's account work outside the Mail app. It keeps mailboxes syncing and rules running in the background, which is how new mail and its notification can arrive with Mail's window closed."),
        ("managedappdistributionagent", "An agent in the device management stack that installs and updates apps pushed by an MDM server. It only has work on a Mac enrolled with a school or employer, and it is what makes required apps appear without anyone downloading them."),
        ("mapspushd", "A daemon in the Maps stack that maintains the push connection Maps relies on. It receives server-initiated updates such as transit and traffic changes and shared ETA information, and delivers them to Maps and related features."),
        ("mdbulkimport", "A support tool of the Metadata framework, the code behind Spotlight. It exists to shovel large batches of metadata into the index at once, so it shows up during a full reindex or after a big import and disappears when the batch is done."),
        ("mdmclient", "The client side of Mobile Device Management. On an enrolled Mac it checks in with the organization's MDM server, applies configuration profiles and restrictions, and reports status; on an unenrolled personal Mac it has essentially nothing to do."),
        ("mdwrite", "A short-lived Metadata framework helper that writes metadata into the Spotlight index. It is spawned to record attributes for files as they change, so you may see it flicker into existence during ordinary file activity."),
        ("mediaremoteagent", "The per-user agent of MediaRemote, the framework that models what is playing. It tracks Now Playing state for your session and routes transport commands, so it is behind the media controls in Control Center and the play and pause keys on your keyboard."),
        ("mediaremoted", "The system-level MediaRemote daemon. It is the shared hub for Now Playing information and remote control commands across processes and devices, which is how a Remote app, a paired iPhone, or AirPlay can drive playback on this Mac."),
        ("mobileactivationd", "The daemon that manages this device's activation state and activation record with Apple's servers. On a Mac it is about the device being validly activated and unlocked for use rather than about cellular service; it mostly runs at setup and after a restore."),
        ("mobileassetd", "The daemon behind MobileAsset, the mechanism macOS uses to download pieces of itself separately from a full OS update. Dictation voices, dictionaries, fonts, Siri data, and Apple Intelligence model files all arrive through it in the background."),
        ("modelcatalogd", "A daemon in the ModelCatalog runtime that keeps the inventory of on-device Apple Intelligence models. It tracks which models and adapters exist, their versions, and where they live, so other components can ask for a capability rather than a file."),
        ("modelmanagerd", "The manager for on-device Apple Intelligence models. It handles loading models into memory, keeping them current, and evicting them under memory pressure, which is why it can hold a noticeable amount of memory while a generative feature is in use."),
        ("neagent", "An agent for Network Extension, the framework third-party VPNs and content filters are built on. It hosts the provider code supplied by an app such as a VPN client, so it appears when a VPN or filter from the App Store is configured and connected."),
        ("nearbyd", "The proximity daemon behind AirDrop, Handoff, and related continuity features. It fuses Bluetooth and Wi-Fi signals, and Ultra Wideband on Macs that have it, to work out which of your devices are close by and roughly where."),
        ("nesessionmanager", "The Network Extension session manager. It owns the lifecycle of VPN and content filter sessions: starting them, tearing them down, applying on-demand rules, and reconnecting after a network change or a wake from sleep."),
        ("networkserviceproxy", "A background daemon in Apple's networking stack that brokers proxied connections on the system's behalf. It is the piece that carries traffic through Apple-operated relays, most visibly iCloud Private Relay, so it wakes whenever apps route requests through those services."),
        ("nfcd", "The system daemon that owns the Near Field Communication hardware interface. It handles tap-to-pay and NFC tag reading on devices that have a reader; on a Mac there is usually nothing to talk to, so it sits idle at essentially zero CPU."),
        ("node", "The Node.js JavaScript runtime, running as a plain command-line process. It is either a development server or build tool you started yourself, or a background helper bundled inside an app like an editor, chat client, or Electron program."),
        ("node_repl", "A Node.js runtime binary launched in interactive REPL mode, usually from a private copy of Node bundled inside an application. It exists so a program can execute JavaScript on the fly rather than running a fixed script file."),
        ("notifyd", "The daemon behind the low-level notify() messaging API that nearly every part of macOS uses. Processes post and subscribe to simple named signals through it, which is how things like time-zone changes or preference updates ripple across the system instantly."),
        ("oahd", "The Rosetta 2 translation daemon on Apple silicon. It converts Intel x86 code into ARM instructions ahead of and during execution, and caches the result, so it spikes the first time you launch an Intel app and quiets down afterwards."),
        ("parentalcontrolsd", "The daemon enforcing Screen Time and parental-control policy for your account. It tracks app and website usage against your limits and is what puts up the time-limit screen or blocks restricted content when a rule trips."),
        ("passd", "The Wallet daemon that stores and maintains your passes: boarding passes, tickets, loyalty and payment cards. It handles updates pushed by issuers and keeps the pass set in sync with your other Apple devices."),
        ("pbs", "The pasteboard server, the system process that owns the clipboard. Every copy and paste passes through it, and it also manages the Services menu, so it briefly wakes each time you press Command-C or Command-V."),
        ("peopled", "A background daemon in the Contacts and suggestions stack. It looks at how you communicate to propose people and contact details you have not saved yet, which is where the suggested recipients in Mail and Messages come from."),
        ("perl", "The Perl interpreter running as a command-line process. Something started it: a shell script, an installer, or a system maintenance task. It is a tool being used rather than a service, so it should exit when its work finishes."),
        ("postersyncd", "A sync daemon for the poster system that backs lock-screen and wallpaper setups. It keeps the wallpapers and their linked photo assets consistent across the Apple devices signed into your account, so it wakes mainly after you change a wallpaper."),
        ("powerdatad", "A daemon in the power-management stack that records battery and energy-use history. Its data is what feeds the battery health and usage graphs in System Settings, and it accumulates quietly in the background all day."),
        ("ps", "The standard command-line tool that prints a snapshot of running processes. It is not a service; it runs for a fraction of a second and exits, so seeing it means a script or another program just asked what was running."),
        ("quit-on-close", "A user-installed background agent, not an Apple component. It watches for an app's last window closing and then quits that app, restoring the behaviour some people prefer over macOS leaving an app running with no windows."),
        ("recentsd", "A daemon that maintains the record of recently used documents and activities for your account. It backs recent-items lists and feeds Handoff and Continuity, so your recent work can be picked up from another Apple device."),
        ("remindd", "The background daemon for the Reminders app. It stores your lists locally, syncs them through iCloud, and schedules the time and location triggers that make a reminder alert fire even when Reminders is not open."),
        ("remotemanagementd", "The daemon implementing Apple Remote Desktop and remote-management access to this Mac. It only does real work if screen sharing or remote management has been enabled, and it is how an administrator can observe or control the machine."),
        ("remotepairingd", "A daemon that handles trusted pairing with other Apple devices over the network. It is what lets Xcode connect to an iPhone or iPad wirelessly for development and debugging, without the device being plugged in."),
        ("routined", "The location-learning daemon behind Significant Locations. It quietly builds an on-device model of the places you go and when, which is what lets Maps predict your commute and Siri suggest leaving for an appointment."),
        ("rtcreportingd", "A reporting daemon for Apple's real-time communication stack. It gathers call-quality metrics from FaceTime and similar live audio and video sessions, so it is generally only active while a call is in progress or just after one ends."),
        ("searchpartyuseragent", "The user-level agent for the Find My network. It manages the encrypted Bluetooth identifiers this Mac broadcasts and the sightings it reports, which is how the crowd-sourced network locates lost Apple devices and AirTags."),
        ("secd", "The keychain daemon that handles secure storage and iCloud Keychain sync. It manages the encrypted exchange of passwords and keys between your trusted devices, and it works with the Secure Enclave to keep secrets off ordinary disk."),
        ("secinitd", "A small daemon in the sandbox subsystem that sets up a process's security context as it launches. Every sandboxed app briefly talks to it at startup to have its restrictions applied, then never needs it again."),
        ("securityd_system", "The system-level half of macOS security services, running outside your login session. It handles the system keychain and core cryptographic operations for daemons that start before anyone logs in, such as Wi-Fi and certificate validation."),
        ("sed", "The classic stream editor, a command-line tool that applies text substitutions line by line. It was almost certainly launched by a shell script doing text processing and lives for only a moment before exiting."),
        ("sharedfilelistd", "The daemon behind the Shared File List API, which stores ordered lists of items on your behalf. It is what remembers your recent documents and applications, your Finder sidebar favourites, and the login items that open at startup."),
        ("simdiskimaged", "A helper daemon from the Xcode toolchain that manages disk images for the iOS Simulator. It attaches the storage a simulated device runs from, so it appears only when you have simulators running or Xcode installed."),
        ("siriappintentsd", "A daemon in the App Intents subsystem that catalogues the actions apps expose. It is what lets Siri and the Shortcuts app discover and run things like Send a message or Start a timer inside third-party apps."),
        ("siriknowledged", "A background daemon holding Siri's on-device knowledge and understanding data. It supports answering questions and resolving what you mean locally, so common requests do not always need a round trip to Apple's servers."),
        ("skhd", "A third-party hotkey daemon installed via Homebrew, not part of macOS. It listens for global key combinations you configured in a text file and runs a shell command when one is pressed, which is how custom system-wide shortcuts work."),
        ("sleep", "The trivial command-line utility that does nothing for a set number of seconds and then exits. Seeing it means a shell script is pacing itself, waiting between retries or for something else to finish."),
        ("sort", "The standard command-line tool that orders lines of text. It is a short-lived step in someone's pipeline, launched by a script or another command, and it uses CPU only while the data is passing through."),
        ("speechmaintenanced", "A maintenance daemon for the on-device speech stack. It downloads, updates, and prunes the voice and recognition models used by Dictation and text-to-speech, so it tends to run after a system update or a language change."),
        ("spotlightknowledged.importer", "An importer process for the Spotlight knowledge store, the semantic layer that sits alongside the classic file index. It reads app and system data into that store so Spotlight can answer richer queries; it runs in bursts and then exits."),
        ("suhelperd", "A privileged helper daemon for Software Update. It handles the parts of checking for, downloading, and staging macOS updates that need elevated rights, which is why it appears around update checks and installations."),
        ("swcd", "The shared web credentials daemon. It fetches and verifies the association files websites publish to prove they belong with a given app, which is what makes password AutoFill offer the right login and universal links open in an app."),
        ("sysmond", "A monitoring daemon that samples per-process resource usage on behalf of other tools. It exists so utilities can read CPU, memory, and I/O statistics without each one needing privileged access to the kernel's accounting."),
        ("systemstats", "A daemon that continuously records system-wide performance and power statistics into a local database. It is the source behind Activity Monitor's history and the energy figures in battery reports, and it writes a little all the time."),
        ("systemstatusd", "A daemon that tracks system status conditions and publishes them to the interface. It backs the state shown in menu bar and Control Centre indicators such as battery, Wi-Fi, and recording-in-use, keeping them consistent across apps."),
        ("t3-resource-monitor", "A helper binary bundled inside the T3 Code application, not a macOS component. The app launches it to watch its own CPU and memory use, and it exits when the app does."),
        ("talagentd", "The Transparent App Lifecycle agent. It saves each app's open windows and their contents so they can be restored exactly where you left them after a quit, a restart, or the system suspending an idle app."),
        ("taskgated", "The gatekeeper for debugging privileges. Whenever a process asks for control over another one, as a debugger or profiler does, taskgated checks its code-signing entitlements and decides whether to allow it."),
        ("taskgated-helper", "A short-lived helper that assists taskgated with entitlement checks. It handles the per-request signature validation work, so it appears briefly whenever a debugger or profiling tool tries to attach to a process."),
        ("textcontextd", "A daemon in the text-input stack that maintains the context behind predictive typing. It learns from what you write to improve inline suggestions and autocorrect, keeping that model on the device rather than sending it away."),
        ("textunderstandingd", "A daemon for on-device text recognition and analysis. It powers Live Text, so it does the work when you select words in a photo or screenshot, and it also detects things like dates and addresses that become tappable."),
        ("tipsd", "The background daemon for the Tips app. It fetches and schedules the suggestion cards Apple sends about new features, which is why an occasional Tips notification appears after a major system update."),
        ("translationd", "The daemon backing the system Translate service. It runs on-device translation for the Translate app and for the Translate option in Safari and other apps' text menus, and it manages the downloaded language packs."),
        ("universalAccessAuthWarn", "A tiny helper that shows the accessibility permission alert. It is the process behind the dialog saying an app wants to control your Mac using accessibility features; it appears only for that prompt and quits once you answer."),
        ("usbmuxd", "The USB multiplexing daemon for iOS devices. It carries multiple logical connections over a single cable or Wi-Fi link, which is how Finder, Xcode, and syncing tools all talk to a connected iPhone or iPad at once."),
        ("useractivityd", "The daemon that publishes and receives user activities for Handoff. It advertises what you are currently doing so a nearby iPhone or iPad can offer to continue it, and it picks up the same signal in the other direction."),
        ("usermanagerd", "A daemon that manages user sessions on the machine. It sets up and tears down the environment for each logged-in account and coordinates fast user switching, so it is busiest at login, logout, and when you switch users."),
        ("usernotificationsd", "The delivery daemon for the modern UserNotifications framework. It receives alerts from apps, applies your Focus and per-app settings, schedules anything time-based, and hands the survivors to Notification Center to display."),
        ("wifip2pd", "The daemon for Wi-Fi peer-to-peer links, including Apple's AWDL protocol. It creates direct device-to-device Wi-Fi connections without a router, which is what carries the actual file data in AirDrop and the stream in AirPlay."),
        ("xprotectd", "A daemon in Apple's built-in XProtect anti-malware system. It schedules and runs the background scans that look for known malicious software already on the Mac and remove it, working silently unless something is found."),
        ("zsh", "The Z shell, macOS's default command interpreter, running as a process. It is either your Terminal session or a script that some app or scheduled job invoked; long-lived zsh processes with steady CPU usually mean a script is looping."),
        ("kernel_task", "The kernel itself, plus its threads, shown as a single process. It handles memory, drivers, and scheduling, and it also deliberately consumes CPU time to keep other work off hot cores, so high kernel_task usage often means the Mac is managing heat."),
        ("WindowServer", "The core graphics server that owns the display. Every app hands it window contents to compose onto the screen, so its CPU use tracks how much is moving: animations, video, scrolling, and extra displays all push it up."),
        ("launchd", "Process ID 1, the first thing the kernel starts and the parent of everything else. It launches daemons and agents on demand, restarts them when they crash, and handles system startup and shutdown ordering."),
        ("loginwindow", "The process that owns your graphical login session. It draws the login screen, starts your session's apps and agents after you authenticate, and coordinates logging out, restarting, and shutting down."),
        ("Finder", "The file manager and desktop shell. It draws the desktop icons and every Finder window, and because it is always running it also handles things like ejecting volumes and the Go To Folder and file-copy interfaces."),
        ("Dock", "The app behind more of the interface than its name suggests. Besides the Dock itself it draws Mission Control, Spaces and the app switcher, plus Stage Manager and Launchpad where present, so it wakes during those animations."),
        ("SystemUIServer", "The older host process for menu bar extras. It draws the status items on the right side of the menu bar that have not moved into Control Center, and restarting it is the classic fix for a menu bar icon that has gone missing."),
        ("ControlCenter", "The app that draws Control Center and the menu bar items it now owns, such as Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, sound, and Focus. It stays resident so those controls respond instantly, and it wakes when you click one."),
        ("NotificationCenter", "The app that presents notifications on screen: the banners in the corner, the notification list, and the widgets alongside it. It receives already-filtered alerts from the notification daemon and is responsible for showing them."),
        ("coreaudiod", "The Core Audio daemon, the hub every sound on the Mac passes through. It mixes streams from all apps, manages input and output devices, and enforces the sample rate, so audio glitches and device switching problems usually point here."),
        ("mds", "The metadata server at the heart of Spotlight. It coordinates indexing of files and their contents and answers search queries, so it is what makes Spotlight, Finder search, and Mail search return results instantly."),
        ("mds_stores", "The worker process that actually reads files and writes Spotlight's index. It is the one that gets busy after you copy a large folder, install software, or attach a new drive, and it settles down once the backlog is indexed."),
        ("mdworker", "A short-lived Spotlight worker spawned by the metadata server to index one batch of files. It loads the importer plugin for each file type and pulls out text and metadata; it appears in bursts after you copy files or install apps, then exits."),
        ("mdworker_shared", "The reusable form of the Spotlight indexing worker, kept alive to handle many files instead of being launched per file. It runs the same sandboxed importer plugins that read a document and hand its text and metadata to the Spotlight index."),
        ("corespotlightd", "The daemon behind the CoreSpotlight API, which indexes content apps hand over directly rather than files on disk. It is why searching Spotlight can surface a message, a mail thread, or an item inside an app, not just filenames."),
        ("spotlightknowledged", "The knowledge layer built on top of the Spotlight index. It relates indexed items to each other and to your activity so search can rank results and offer suggestions, rather than just matching text; it mostly works in the background."),
        ("backupd", "The Time Machine backup daemon. It scans for what changed since the last snapshot, copies it to your backup disk or network destination, and thins old backups; heavy disk and CPU use here usually means a backup is running right now."),
        ("fseventsd", "The file system events daemon in the kernel's notification path. It writes a continuous log of directory changes for each volume so Time Machine and Spotlight can ask what changed since a point in time instead of rescanning everything."),
        ("opendirectoryd", "The Open Directory daemon that answers every user, group, and password lookup on the Mac. Local accounts, login, sudo, and any corporate LDAP or Active Directory binding all resolve through it, so it is consulted constantly by other processes."),
        ("securityd", "The daemon behind the Security framework and your keychains. It holds decrypted secrets in its own process instead of handing them to apps, brokers access to keys and certificates, and is what stands behind a prompt asking to allow keychain access."),
        ("trustd", "The certificate trust evaluation daemon in the Security stack. Whenever an app or Safari opens a TLS connection, trustd checks the certificate chain against the system trust store and revocation data and returns a verdict, which is why it wakes with network activity."),
        ("cfprefsd", "The Core Foundation preferences daemon that owns every app's settings plist. Apps do not write their preference files directly; they ask cfprefsd, which caches and coalesces writes so settings survive crashes and stay consistent between processes."),
        ("distnoted", "The distributed notification center daemon. It relays small broadcast messages between separate processes, so when one app changes something system-wide, other running apps hear about it and can update themselves."),
        ("diskarbitrationd", "The Disk Arbitration daemon that supervises volumes appearing and disappearing. It decides whether and where a disk mounts, notifies interested apps, and coordinates ejects; it is what lets an app veto unmounting a disk it is still using."),
        ("powerd", "The power management daemon. It tracks sleep and wake, battery and charging state, and the assertions that apps take out to keep the display or system awake; it is the process that decides when your Mac actually goes to sleep."),
        ("thermalmonitord", "The thermal management daemon. It watches temperature sensors and publishes a thermal pressure level that the system and apps react to, which is what leads to reduced performance or fan ramp-up when the machine is running hot."),
        ("bluetoothd", "The main Bluetooth daemon and owner of the Bluetooth controller. Pairing, connecting, and all traffic to keyboards, mice, headphones, and other accessories flow through it, and it stays running whenever Bluetooth is switched on."),
        ("airportd", "The Wi-Fi daemon behind the menu bar Wi-Fi item. It drives the wireless hardware: scanning for networks, joining and roaming between access points, and handling authentication; periodic activity here is usually a background scan."),
        ("sharingd", "The daemon behind AirDrop, Handoff, and other Continuity sharing features. It advertises your Mac to nearby Apple devices and handles the handshakes for AirDrop transfers, Universal Clipboard, and shared content between your devices."),
        ("rapportd", "The daemon for Apple's Rapport nearby-device link, a companion to sharingd. It discovers and maintains connections to your other Apple devices over Wi-Fi and Bluetooth, which is what makes Continuity features notice a nearby iPhone or iPad."),
        ("nsurlsessiond", "The system daemon that carries out background URLSession transfers on behalf of apps. When an app schedules a download or upload to continue after it quits or sleeps, this process does the actual networking and hands over the result later."),
        ("cloudd", "The CloudKit daemon that talks to iCloud servers for apps. Any app storing records in iCloud, from Notes to third-party apps, sends its sync traffic through cloudd rather than connecting itself, so it is busy when your data is catching up."),
        ("bird", "The iCloud Drive and document sync daemon. It watches your iCloud folders, uploads changes, downloads files you open that are only stored in the cloud, and manages evicting local copies to free space."),
        ("apsd", "The Apple Push Notification service daemon. It holds one long-lived connection to Apple's push servers on behalf of the whole Mac, then routes arriving notifications to the right app, which is far cheaper than every app polling on its own."),
        ("akd", "The AuthKit daemon that handles your Apple Account sign-in. It manages the tokens other services use to prove who you are, and it is the process behind two-factor prompts and requests to re-enter your Apple Account password."),
        ("accountsd", "The Accounts framework daemon that owns the system's account database. It stores the mail, calendar, and other internet accounts you add in System Settings and vends them to apps so each app does not keep its own copy of your credentials."),
        ("syncdefaultsd", "The daemon that syncs small key-value preference data through iCloud. It is why some app settings and lightweight state follow you between your Mac, iPhone, and iPad without the app doing any work itself."),
        ("photoanalysisd", "The background analysis daemon for your Photos library. It scans images to recognise faces, group people, and build Memories; it deliberately prefers to run while the Mac is idle and on power, so it can use noticeable CPU overnight."),
        ("mediaanalysisd", "The media analysis daemon built on Apple's Vision stack. It examines photos and video to detect scenes, objects, and text, which is what powers searching your library by what is in a picture, plus Live Text and Visual Look Up."),
        ("photolibraryd", "The daemon that owns your Photos library database. Photos, and any app asking for your pictures, goes through it for reads and writes, so it runs whenever something is browsing, importing, or syncing your library."),
        ("amfid", "The userspace half of AppleMobileFileIntegrity, the code signing enforcement system. When the kernel meets a program it has not validated, it asks amfid to check the signature and entitlements before allowing the code to run."),
        ("syspolicyd", "The System Policy daemon that implements Gatekeeper. It decides whether a downloaded app is allowed to launch by checking its signature and notarisation ticket with Apple, and it is behind the dialog warning you about an unverified developer."),
        ("XProtectService", "Part of Apple's built-in XProtect anti-malware system. It matches files against Apple's signature rules when something new is launched or downloaded, and it is what blocks a file the system recognises as known malware."),
        ("tccd", "The Transparency, Consent and Control daemon that guards privacy-sensitive resources. Every request for your camera, microphone, contacts, screen recording, or Documents folder passes through it; it shows the permission prompt and remembers your answer."),
        ("sandboxd", "The userspace companion to the kernel's App Sandbox. The actual blocking happens in the kernel, while this daemon handles the reporting side, recording and diagnosing the violations when a sandboxed app tries to reach something it is not allowed to."),
        ("installd", "The daemon that performs app installations, notably from the App Store. It unpacks and places the app bundle, verifies its signature, and sets up its container, so it is active while an install or update is being applied."),
        ("softwareupdated", "The Software Update daemon. It checks Apple's servers for macOS updates, downloads them in the background, and prepares them for installation; a long stretch of network and disk activity here is usually a macOS update downloading."),
        ("storeaccountd", "A CommerceKit daemon that manages your App Store and media account state. It keeps track of which Apple Account is signed in for purchases and vends that identity to the App Store and other store-aware apps."),
        ("appstoreagent", "The background agent of the App Store, separate from the App Store window. It checks for available updates, applies automatic updates if you have them enabled, and reports the badge count, all without the app being open."),
        ("revisiond", "The daemon behind document version history, part of the Generational Storage framework. It stores past revisions of files saved by apps that support Versions, which is what the Revert To and Browse All Versions commands read from."),
        ("coreduetd", "The CoreDuet daemon that collects on-device signals about how and when you use your Mac. Other parts of the system read that history to schedule background work at good moments and to predict what you are likely to want next."),
        ("dasd", "The Duet Activity Scheduler daemon, the system's traffic controller for background maintenance. Apps and daemons register work with it, and it decides when to run each job based on power, thermals, network, and whether you are using the machine."),
        ("duetexpertd", "The prediction component of the CoreDuet stack. It turns the usage history other Duet processes collect into on-device suggestions, such as which app you are likely to open next, without sending that data off the Mac."),
        ("knowledge-agent", "The daemon that maintains the on-device knowledge store of your app and device usage. It is the database that Screen Time, Siri suggestions, and various prediction features read from; it stays local to this Mac and your synced devices."),
        ("contextstored", "The CoreDuet context store daemon. It records short-lived contextual state, things like what is running and what the device is currently doing, so suggestion and scheduling features can ask what is happening right now."),
        ("suggestd", "The daemon that reads content you view in apps such as Mail, Messages, and Safari to spot useful details. It is why macOS offers to add a detected contact, event, or flight to your calendar; the analysis is done locally."),
        ("parsecd", "The client daemon for Apple's Parsec search service, which backs Siri Suggestions and the web and app results in Spotlight. When you type in Spotlight, this is the process that queries Apple's servers for suggestions beyond your local files."),
        ("assistantd", "The main Siri daemon. It handles requests once Siri is invoked, coordinating speech recognition, the round trip to Apple's servers, and running the resulting action; it is idle unless Siri is listening or working on something."),
        ("siriactionsd", "The daemon behind Shortcuts and Siri actions. It holds the catalogue of actions apps have donated to the system and runs them when a shortcut fires, whether you triggered it from the menu bar, Siri, or an automation."),
        ("callservicesd", "The daemon that manages calls on your Mac. It handles FaceTime audio and video and the iPhone cellular calls relayed to the Mac through Continuity, including ringing, answering, and keeping only one call active at a time."),
        ("imagent", "The IMCore agent that runs the Messages connection. It maintains the link to Apple's iMessage servers and handles sending, receiving, typing indicators, and read receipts; the Messages window is only the front end on top of it."),
        ("identityservicesd", "The Identity Services daemon that manages your registration for iMessage, FaceTime, and Continuity. It handles which phone numbers and addresses reach you, and the encryption keys those services use to identify your devices to each other."),
        ("IMDPersistenceAgent", "The agent that owns the Messages database on disk. It writes incoming and outgoing messages and attachments to the local chat store and serves queries from it, so it wakes with message activity and when Messages searches history."),
        ("soagent", "A background agent associated with Mail's account handling, running outside the visible Mail window. It exists so mail account state stays managed by the system even when you have not opened Mail, and it is otherwise idle."),
        ("mailfetchd", "The background mail fetching daemon. It checks configured mail accounts for new messages on a schedule set by the system rather than by Mail being open, so new mail can arrive and notify you without the app running in front of you."),
        ("smd", "The ServiceManagement framework daemon. It installs and manages privileged helper tools and background login items on behalf of apps, which is what stands behind the prompt asking for your password so an app can add a background item."),
        ("kextd", "The historical kernel extension daemon, which loaded drivers into the kernel on request and validated their signatures. On recent versions of macOS that job moved to kernelmanagerd and system extensions, so seeing it running is unusual."),
        ("diskimagesiod", "The userspace I/O helper for mounted disk images. When you open a .dmg or a sparse bundle, this process backs the virtual disk by reading and writing the underlying image file, so it is active while you copy files to or from one."),
        ("hidd", "The Human Interface Device daemon that sits between input hardware and the window server. Keyboards, trackpads, mice, and game controllers report through it, and it turns raw device reports into the events apps receive."),
        ("universalaccessd", "The daemon behind the Accessibility features in System Settings. It backs things like Zoom, display filters, Switch Control, and other assistive options, and coordinates them with the window server so they apply across every app."),
        ("ScreenSaverEngine", "The process that actually draws the screen saver, loading whichever saver module you have chosen. It launches when the screen saver starts and quits when you dismiss it, and its GPU use reflects how animated your chosen saver is."),
        ("screencaptureui", "The interface for taking screenshots and screen recordings. It draws the crosshair, the selection rectangle, the Command-Shift-5 toolbar, and the floating thumbnail afterwards, then exits once you have saved or dismissed the capture."),
        ("QuickLookUIService", "The XPC service that renders the Quick Look preview window itself. It runs the preview out of process so a malformed document can only crash the preview rather than Finder, and it appears when you press Space on a file."),
        ("quicklookd", "The Quick Look daemon that generates previews and thumbnails by running the appropriate generator plugin for each file type. It is what fills in the icon previews you see in Finder as well as the content of a Quick Look window."),
        ("iconservicesagent", "The per-user side of Icon Services. It resolves the right icon for an app, document, or volume and hands it to Finder, the Dock, and other apps, drawing on the shared icon cache rather than rendering each icon repeatedly."),
        ("iconservicesd", "The system-wide Icon Services daemon that maintains the shared icon cache. It stores rendered icons at the sizes apps request so browsing a folder does not mean re-extracting artwork from every bundle and document each time."),
        ("lsd", "The Launch Services daemon that owns the database mapping file types and URL schemes to applications. It decides which app opens a document when you double-click it, populates the Open With menu, and updates when you install or move apps."),
        ("pkd", "The PlugInKit daemon that manages app extensions. It discovers extensions inside installed app bundles, validates them, and decides which ones may run, which is why an extension only appears in System Settings after pkd has accepted it."),
        ("pluginkit", "The command line tool for the same PlugInKit extension system that pkd runs. It registers, removes, and lists app extensions, and is generally run briefly by the system or by an installer rather than staying resident."),
        ("nehelper", "A helper daemon for the NetworkExtension framework, which covers VPNs, content filters, and DNS proxies. Apps ask it about the configured network extensions and their status, so it is consulted whenever VPN or filter state is checked."),
        ("networkd", "A low-level networking daemon that backs parts of the system's network stack for apps and services. It handles connection plumbing on behalf of higher-level frameworks, so it stirs whenever traffic flows and otherwise sits idle in the background."),
        ("mDNSResponder", "The system-wide DNS daemon, launched at boot, implementing both ordinary unicast DNS lookups and Multicast DNS with service discovery (Bonjour). Every hostname your Mac resolves goes through it, and it is what makes AirPlay speakers, network printers, and shared Macs appear by name locally."),
        ("configd", "The System Configuration daemon, one of the oldest core services in macOS. It tracks the desired and current state of network settings; interfaces, addresses, DNS servers, and proxies, and notifies apps the moment something changes, such as when you join a Wi-Fi network."),
        ("symptomsd", "The daemon behind Apple's Symptom framework, which watches network behavior for signs of trouble. It gathers per-connection quality signals so the system can tell when Wi-Fi has gone bad and decide to fall back to another network."),
        ("netbiosd", "A daemon implementing the legacy NetBIOS protocol used by Windows networking. It registers and defends your Mac's NetBIOS name and browses the local network for others, feeding those names back through mDNSResponder so Windows machines show up in the Finder sidebar."),
        ("socketfilterfw", "The Application Firewall daemon, which filters inbound connections on a per-application basis rather than per-port. It enforces the on/off switch and the app allow list in System Settings, and it is what raises the prompt asking whether an app may accept incoming connections."),
        ("logd", "The daemon at the center of the unified logging system, handling messages emitted by every process through os_log. It routes live log streams, decides what gets persisted, and manages the on-disk log archive that Console and the `log` command read from."),
        ("syslogd", "The older Apple System Log server, still present alongside the newer unified logging system. It receives messages from legacy code paths and BSD-style syslog clients and writes them to files such as /var/log; on a modern Mac it is usually near-idle."),
        ("aslmanager", "A short-lived maintenance tool for Apple System Log data, invoked periodically rather than running all the time. It rotates, compresses, archives, and expires old log files so logging cannot slowly fill your disk; seeing it briefly is normal housekeeping."),
        ("UserEventAgent", "A launchd companion that hosts small system plugins in a shared process. It handles high-level events launchd cannot watch on its own; things like devices appearing or user session changes, which is why several unrelated bits of macOS live inside this one process."),
        ("gamecontrollerd", "The daemon behind the GameController framework. It discovers connected gamepads and arbitrates which app currently owns them, so a PlayStation or Xbox controller paired over Bluetooth is recognized consistently across games."),
        ("audioanalyticsd", "An analytics daemon scoped to the audio subsystem. It aggregates statistics about audio routing and playback behavior for Apple's diagnostics pipeline; it is not recording sound, and it only submits data if you have opted into sharing analytics."),
        ("analyticsd", "The general diagnostics and usage analytics daemon. It collects the aggregated crash and usage measurements that appear under Privacy and Security, Analytics and Improvements, and it submits nothing unless you have turned that sharing on."),
        ("ReportCrash", "A helper that launches only when another process crashes. It analyzes the dying process, records what code was running and why it failed, and writes a crash report into the Console app; it exits as soon as the report is saved."),
        ("spindump", "A whole-system sampling profiler that other parts of macOS invoke automatically. It runs when an app stops responding, or when you force quit a beach-balled app, and captures a snapshot of what every process was doing so the hang can be diagnosed later."),
        ("sysdiagnose", "The bundled diagnostic collector that gathers logs, configuration, and system state into a single archive for Apple support or a bug report. It is heavy while it runs but lasts only minutes, and normally appears because you or a support workflow triggered it."),
        ("com.apple.WebKit.WebContent", "A sandboxed WebKit content process, one per web page or web view. It runs that page's HTML, CSS, and JavaScript in isolation, so a runaway site burns only its own process; this is what to check when one tab eats CPU."),
        ("com.apple.WebKit.Networking", "The shared WebKit networking process that fetches resources on behalf of all web content processes. Keeping loads, caching, and cookies out of the page's own sandbox means a compromised page cannot reach the network directly."),
        ("com.apple.WebKit.GPU", "The WebKit GPU process, which owns graphics and media work for web views. Page rendering, canvas, WebGL, and video decoding are done here rather than inside the sandboxed content process, so it is often the busiest WebKit process while you watch video."),
        ("Safari", "The main Safari application process; the browser UI itself, holding windows, tabs, toolbars, and history. The actual web pages run in separate WebKit processes, so this one is usually light unless the interface is doing work."),
        ("SafariBookmarksSyncAgent", "A background agent that reconciles Safari bookmarks and Reading List with iCloud. It wakes when you add or reorder a bookmark, or when a change arrives from another device, and is how your bookmarks stay identical on your iPhone and Mac."),
        ("com.apple.Safari.SandboxBroker", "A broker process that mediates requests from Safari's tightly sandboxed components. When a web page needs something the sandbox forbids by default, such as access to a file you chose to upload, the request is vetted here rather than granted directly."),
        ("swtransparencyd", "A daemon supporting Apple's software transparency system, which publishes cryptographic evidence about the software and keys the system trusts. It checks that what your Mac is asked to trust matches a publicly verifiable log, and it runs briefly and rarely."),
        ("remoted", "The Remote Service Discovery daemon. It advertises and finds services offered by nearby or attached devices, which is part of how a connected iPhone or another Mac becomes visible to developer tools and system features."),
        ("usbd", "A userspace daemon for the USB subsystem that handles device attach and detach on behalf of the kernel's USB stack. It briefly does work whenever you plug in a drive, hub, keyboard, or charger, and then falls quiet."),
        ("ionodecache", "A small CoreServices helper that maintains a cached view of the I/O Registry, the kernel's live tree of hardware and drivers. Keeping that data cached spares apps from repeatedly walking the registry when they ask what hardware is present."),
        ("PerfPowerServices", "A background service that collects performance and power telemetry, including battery health and energy-use statistics. It is the source of the battery condition and usage history shown in System Settings, and it samples slowly rather than continuously."),
        ("wifivelocityd", "A diagnostics daemon in the Wi-Fi stack. It captures detailed radio and association information when the system detects a connection problem, providing the data behind Wireless Diagnostics and Wi-Fi bug reports."),
        ("wifianalyticsd", "A daemon that aggregates statistics about Wi-Fi performance and connection behavior over time. It exists to characterize how well wireless is working rather than to manage the connection, and it only shares data if analytics are enabled."),
        ("locationd", "The Location Services daemon. It fuses Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, and any GPS-like signals into a position estimate and enforces which apps and daemons are allowed to ask for it; the arrow in the menu bar means locationd just served a request."),
        ("geod", "A background daemon for Apple Maps data. It fetches, caches, and maintains map tiles and related geographic resources so Maps and any app showing a map view can draw without re-downloading everything."),
        ("timed", "The modern time synchronization daemon. It merges inputs from network time sources, tracks how uncertain the current clock reading is, and adjusts the clock while staying aware of power and battery conditions so it does not wake the machine needlessly."),
        ("ntpd", "The classic Network Time Protocol daemon, which talks to internet time servers and steers the clock toward them. On current macOS this role is largely handled by timed, so seeing ntpd is unusual and typically means something explicitly enabled it."),
        ("cupsd", "The CUPS print scheduler, the open-source printing system Apple maintains and ships with macOS. It manages printer queues, converts documents into what the printer understands, and speaks IPP over the network; it starts on demand when you print or add a printer."),
        ("PrinterProxy", "A small per-printer application that macOS generates for each printer you add. It represents that printer's queue in the Dock and lets you pause, resume, or cancel jobs; it appears while a print job is active and quits afterward."),
        ("TextInputMenuAgent", "The agent that owns the input-source menu in the menu bar; the flag or character showing your current keyboard layout. It runs in your login session and only does work when you open that menu or change languages."),
        ("TextInputSwitcher", "A helper in the text input system that handles switching between keyboard layouts and input methods. It is what responds when you press the input-source shortcut or pick a different language, including the overlay that appears while you hold the key."),
        ("keyboardservicesd", "A daemon backing keyboard-related text services such as text replacement shortcuts and learned typing data, kept in sync across your devices through iCloud. It is why expanding a snippet you defined on your iPhone also works on your Mac."),
        ("AutoFillHeuristicValidator", "A helper process in the password AutoFill system. It evaluates the rules used to recognize sign-in and payment fields on a page so AutoFill offers the right credential in the right box; it runs occasionally and briefly rather than continuously."),
        ("SecurityAgent", "The trusted UI process that draws authentication dialogs on behalf of the security system. Any window asking for your account password, Touch ID, or an admin unlock is drawn by SecurityAgent, deliberately kept separate from the app making the request."),
        ("authd", "The authorization daemon that decides whether a given right may be granted; for example, whether unlocking a settings pane requires an administrator. It evaluates the policy database and asks SecurityAgent to prompt you when a credential is needed."),
        ("coreservicesd", "A long-running daemon providing shared services to the CoreServices frameworks that nearly every app links against. It handles foundational bookkeeping such as app and document type registration; it must not be terminated and is never meant to be launched by hand."),
        ("runningboardd", "The assertion-services daemon that governs process lifecycle across macOS. Other components take assertions through it to say a process must stay running, stay awake, or may be suspended, which is how the system decides what to throttle when you switch away from an app."),
        ("usernoted", "The Notification Center daemon in your login session. It receives notifications from apps and from Apple's push service, stores them, decides whether they are shown, silenced, or held by a Focus, and draws the banners and the Notification Center list."),
        ("WeatherWidget", "The process hosting the Weather widget on your desktop or in Notification Center. It periodically fetches the forecast for its configured location and redraws; each widget runs outside the main app so a stuck one cannot take down the rest."),
        ("spotlightknowledged.updater", "A background updater for the on-device knowledge store that feeds Spotlight suggestions. It refreshes the data used to enrich search results and runs opportunistically, typically when the Mac is idle or on power."),
        ("linkd", "The App Intents daemon, also known as AppIntentsDaemon. It indexes the actions apps publish about themselves and brokers calls into them, which is what lets Siri and Shortcuts run an app's actions without opening the app."),
        ("DockDoor", "A third-party open-source app, not part of macOS, that adds Windows-style hover previews to the Dock. It watches the Dock and your open windows so that pointing at an app icon shows thumbnails of its windows; quitting it removes that feature only."),
        ("MenuBarAgent", "A per-session agent that participates in drawing and managing the system menu bar at the top of the screen. It works alongside the window server and Control Center, and it becomes briefly busy when menu bar contents change."),
        ("watchdogd", "A health-monitoring daemon that checks the system is still making forward progress. If it or the kernel watchdog concludes things are wedged, it tries to correct the problem and, as a last resort, will panic and restart the Mac rather than leave it hung."),
        ("nsurlstoraged", "The storage daemon behind Foundation's URL loading system. It owns the shared HTTP cache and cookie store that apps read and write through NSURLSession, so it is doing work whenever apps fetch web content in the background."),
        ("trustdFileHelper", "A privileged helper for trustd, the certificate trust daemon. It performs the on-disk updates trustd itself is not permitted to make, such as installing refreshed root certificate and revocation data; it runs briefly and rarely."),
        ("MTLCompilerService", "An out-of-process compiler for the Metal graphics framework. It turns an app's GPU shader source into code your specific graphics chip can run, which is why it spikes for a few seconds when a game or graphics-heavy app launches or loads a new scene."),
        ("VTDecoderXPCService", "An XPC service of the VideoToolbox framework that performs video decoding, usually on dedicated media hardware. Playing an H.264 or HEVC video in almost any app routes frames through here, which keeps decoding off the CPU and out of the app's own process."),
        ("VTEncoderXPCService", "The VideoToolbox counterpart that performs video encoding, again typically on the media engine rather than the CPU. It appears while you record the screen, export from a video editor, or share a camera feed in a call."),
        ("AMPDeviceDiscoveryAgent", "An agent that watches for iPhones, iPads, and iPods appearing over USB or Wi-Fi so the media and device-sync stack can offer to work with them. It is why a connected iPhone shows up in the Finder sidebar and in Music."),
        ("AMPLibraryAgent", "The background agent that maintains your Music library database. It imports and updates track metadata, artwork, and playlists, and reconciles changes with Apple Music, so it can be busy after adding songs even when the Music app is closed."),
        ("Music", "The Music application itself; playback, browsing, and the Apple Music storefront. Much of the library bookkeeping happens outside it in AMPLibraryAgent, so heavy CPU use here usually means the interface, artwork, or streaming is doing the work."),
        ("cloudphotod", "The daemon that synchronizes your photo library with iCloud Photos. It uploads new pictures, downloads full-resolution originals when you open an optimized thumbnail, and reconciles albums and edits; it can run long and hot after a large import."),
        ("adprivacyd", "A small daemon managing the advertising-related privacy settings, including personalized ads and the identifier used for them. It enforces your choices under Privacy and Security rather than serving any ads itself."),
        ("seld", "The Siri event-learning daemon. It builds on-device models of your routines from your activity so Siri and the system can make timely suggestions; the work stays local and is tied to the Siri and Search privacy settings."),
        ("proactived", "The daemon behind proactive suggestions; the anticipatory hints macOS offers, such as suggested apps, shortcuts, and Spotlight suggestions. It correlates on-device signals about how and when you use things, and does the bulk of its work while the Mac is idle."),
        ("homed", "The HomeKit daemon. It keeps your home's accessories, rooms, scenes, and automations in sync and communicates with hubs and devices on your network, which is what lets the Home app and Siri control lights and locks."),
        ("findmydeviced", "The device side of Find My. It maintains your Mac's registration with the service, reports location when permitted, and carries out remote actions such as playing a sound or marking the machine as lost."),
        ("searchpartyd", "The daemon implementing the crowd-sourced Find My network. It broadcasts and listens for the rotating encrypted Bluetooth beacons that let any nearby Apple device anonymously relay the position of a lost item or Mac, including AirTags."),
        ("familycircled", "A daemon that maintains your Family Sharing group membership and the state shared across it. It keeps track of who is in the family and what is shared, such as purchases, subscriptions, and location sharing between members."),
        ("screentimed", "The system daemon for Screen Time. It records app and website usage, enforces limits and downtime, and syncs that history through iCloud so usage from your other devices is counted in the same totals."),
        ("ScreenTimeAgent", "The per-user agent counterpart to the Screen Time daemon, running inside your login session. It observes what is in use in your session and presents the user-facing side of limits, including the screen that appears when an app's time is up."),
        ("UsageTrackingAgent", "A per-user launchd agent in the Screen Time stack. It records how long you spend in each app and website and enforces the limits and downtime schedules you set, which is where the you have reached your limit notices come from."),
        ("CommCenter", "The telephony daemon macOS inherits from iOS. On a Mac it handles the phone-call side of Continuity, so it is what actually places and receives calls and text messages relayed through your iPhone."),
        ("wirelessproxd", "A background daemon for Apple's wireless proximity features. It watches the short-range radio chatter nearby Apple devices broadcast, which is how Continuity features and automatic pairing know a device is close by."),
        ("sharekit-helper", "A helper process for ShareKit, the framework behind the system Share menu. It runs when you click Share so the sheet can list targets like Mail, Messages, AirDrop and third-party share extensions, and it goes away once you are done."),
        ("ShortcutsActionsService", "A background service belonging to the Shortcuts app. It is where the actions inside a shortcut actually execute, so it appears while a shortcut is running, whether you started it from the app, the menu bar, or a keyboard shortcut."),
        ("WorkflowExtensionsService", "A companion service in the Shortcuts and Automator machinery. It hosts shortcut actions and Quick Actions that apps provide, running them out of process on the system's behalf and exiting once the automation finishes."),
        ("AirPlayXPCHelper", "A privileged launchd daemon in the AirPlay stack. It handles discovery and session setup for streaming to an Apple TV or AirPlay speaker, and for receiving AirPlay from an iPhone or iPad onto this Mac."),
        ("replayd", "The ReplayKit daemon on macOS. It implements in-app screen capture, recording and broadcast, so it wakes up when something is recording or streaming the screen rather than running all the time."),
        ("coremediad", "A daemon in the CoreMedia stack, the layer underneath AVFoundation that moves audio and video samples around. It is generally busy only while apps are decoding, playing or capturing media."),
        ("avconferenced", "The daemon behind Apple's audio and video conferencing stack. It sets up and runs FaceTime calls, negotiating the connection and handling related Continuity pieces such as using your iPhone as a camera."),
        ("appleh13camerad", "A userspace driver daemon for the image signal processor built into Apple silicon. It runs the low-level side of the built-in camera, doing exposure, noise reduction and image processing before frames ever reach an app."),
        ("VDCAssistant", "The CoreMediaIO assistant process for video capture devices. It sits between apps and the built-in FaceTime camera or a USB webcam, and its notable job is letting more than one app capture from the same camera at once."),
        ("cameracaptured", "A background daemon in the camera capture stack. It brokers app access to camera devices on the system and is normally only active while something is actually using a camera."),
        ("audiomxd", "A Core Audio daemon that arbitrates audio routing, including across nearby Apple devices. It is the piece that decides which machine your AirPods should be connected to when your Mac, iPhone and iPad are all in range."),
        ("coreaudiod ", "The central Core Audio daemon; essentially every sound on the Mac passes through it. It loads audio device drivers, mixes the streams from all running apps, and handles the switch when you plug in headphones or connect a speaker."),
        ("OSDUIHelper", "The on-screen display helper inside OSDFramework. It draws the translucent overlay panels for volume, screen brightness and keyboard backlight, appearing for a second when you press those keys and idling the rest of the time."),
        ("TouchBarServer", "The render server for the Touch Bar. It provides the Core Animation drawing services apps use to put controls on the strip; on a Mac with no Touch Bar it has nothing to draw."),
        ("talagent", "The Transparent App Lifecycle agent, part of AppKit's Resume feature. It saves and restores window positions, open documents and scroll state so apps come back looking the way you left them after a quit or a restart."),
        ("pboard", "The pasteboard server, one of the oldest processes in macOS. It holds whatever you have copied and hands it over to apps that paste, and it also backs drag-and-drop and the find pasteboard."),
        ("universalaccessAuthWarn", "A small helper in the accessibility and privacy subsystem. It puts up the dialog asking whether an app should be allowed to control your Mac through Accessibility, and it exists only while that prompt is on screen."),
        ("PowerChime", "A tiny login-time agent living in CoreServices. Its whole job is the chime and the charging indicator when you connect a power adapter, so it sits idle until the power state changes."),
        ("BezelServices", "The legacy bezel service, an older sibling of OSDUIHelper. It handles hardware key feedback such as brightness and keyboard backlight overlays, along with some function-key behavior on Apple keyboards."),
        ("corebrightnessd", "The Core Brightness daemon. It manages display brightness behavior and color temperature, most visibly Night Shift, warming your screen on a schedule or from sunset to sunrise."),
        ("displaypolicyd", "A daemon that enforces graphics power policy. On Macs with both an integrated and a discrete GPU it decides which chip drives the display and when to switch, keeping demanding apps on the fast GPU and everything else on the efficient one."),
        ("GPUToolsAgent", "An agent installed alongside Xcode's GPU developer tools. It lets Xcode and Instruments attach to a running app to capture and inspect its GPU work, so it only matters if someone is debugging graphics code."),
        ("MTLReplayer", "A Metal developer tool that replays captured GPU frames. After a Metal frame capture in Xcode, this process re-runs the recorded draw calls so each one can be stepped through and inspected."),
        ("CMFSyncAgent", "An agent in the CommunicationsFilter framework. It keeps your blocked-caller list and message filtering settings in sync across your Apple devices, so a number you block on your iPhone is blocked here too."),
        ("CVMServer", "The Core Virtual Machine server, a system daemon that runs compile work in a separate process. In practice it compiles OpenGL shaders for apps still on the legacy graphics stack, so it tends to appear briefly when such an app launches."),
        ("ClassroomDeviceExpert", "A component supporting Apple Classroom and managed-device features. It answers queries about what this Mac is and what it can do on behalf of those systems; on a personal Mac with no management enrollment it has essentially nothing to do."),
        ("ContainerMetadataExtractor", "A short-lived helper that reads metadata out of app container folders, such as the per-app directories in iCloud Drive. The system spawns it to work out what an app's documents area contains and it exits as soon as it is done."),
        ("Core Audio Driver (MSTeamsAudioDevice.driver)", "A Core Audio plug-in loaded inside coreaudiod rather than a standalone app. Microsoft Teams installs it as a virtual audio device so it can capture and route call audio, and it shows up in Sound settings next to your real hardware."),
        ("Core Audio Driver (SteamStreamingMicrophone.driver)", "A Core Audio plug-in installed by Steam and loaded inside coreaudiod. It presents a virtual microphone that carries voice from the device you are streaming to during Remote Play, so games and voice chat treat it as an ordinary input."),
        ("Core Audio Driver (SteamStreamingSpeakers.driver)", "A Core Audio plug-in installed by Steam and loaded inside coreaudiod. It presents a virtual output so game audio can be captured and sent to whatever device you are playing on during Remote Play instead of your local speakers."),
        ("DeviceFS", "A service that exposes a connected Apple device's files to the Mac. Developer tools such as Xcode use it to reach data on an attached iPhone or iPad, so it has nothing to do unless a device is plugged in."),
        ("IOMFB_bics_daemon", "A low-level display daemon tied to IOMobileFramebuffer, the framebuffer driver used on Apple silicon. It handles backlight and brightness calibration for the built-in display, keeping the panel consistent as brightness and content change."),
        ("Kinetics", "A third-party menu bar utility, not an Apple process. It overrides the animation durations macOS uses for things like Mission Control and the Dock, which is why switching Spaces and hiding the Dock feel faster than the defaults."),
        ("Launchie", "A third-party app launcher that recreates the old Launchpad grid. It stays resident in the background so it can appear instantly on a hotkey or gesture and show your installed apps as pages of icons."),
        ("MENotificationAgent", "A small agent for the Media Extensions system, which lets apps add support for video and audio formats macOS does not ship with. It tells running apps when such an extension is installed, updated or removed so they can pick up the new format."),
        ("Mac Extension", "A Safari app extension bundled inside Noir, a third-party app. It injects the styling that gives ordinary websites a dark appearance when your Mac is in Dark Mode, and it runs inside Safari's extension sandbox rather than as its own app."),
        ("MacWebExtension", "The Safari extension bundled inside 1Blocker. It supplies Safari with the content-blocking rules that hide ads and trackers; Safari runs it as a sandboxed extension process separate from the 1Blocker app itself."),
        ("MacWidgetExtension", "The widget bundled inside 1Blocker. macOS launches it only long enough to draw the widget's contents for Notification Center or the desktop, showing figures like how much has been blocked, and then puts it away."),
        ("NMHSafari", "A Safari extension shipped with Cold Turkey Blocker. It is how the blocker sees and interrupts pages in Safari, since a normal app cannot inspect browser tabs; the name points to a native messaging host, the usual bridge between a browser and a desktop app."),
        ("NewsTag", "A helper associated with Apple News. It handles tagging and categorizing News content in the background, so it is only doing anything when News articles are being fetched, indexed or read."),
        ("PAH_Extension", "An app extension inside PressAndHold.app, one of the system input methods. It draws the little accent picker that pops up when you hold down a key such as e or a, letting you pick an accented character with a number key or a click."),
        ("PeekXExt", "A Quick Look preview extension bundled inside PeekX, a third-party app. macOS loads it into its own sandboxed process when you press Space on a file so it can render previews the built-in generators do not handle."),
        ("PopupExtension", "The Safari extension that draws AdGuard for Safari's toolbar popup. It runs when you click the AdGuard icon in Safari, showing the per-site blocking controls and counters, and it quits when the popup closes."),
        ("SkyComputerUseService", "A helper service inside Codex's computer-use agent bundle. It provides the screen capture and keyboard and mouse control the agent needs to drive the interface, so it is running while an agent is operating the Mac on your behalf."),
        ("ThemeWidgetControlViewService", "A view service that renders widget and control previews out of process. It is what fills in the thumbnails when you open the widget gallery or the Control Center controls picker, and it exits once those previews are drawn."),
        ("WidgetsExtension", "The widget bundled inside BrightIntosh, a third-party display brightness utility. macOS runs it briefly to draw the widget's contents and then suspends it until the next refresh."),
        ("agentstored", "A daemon in Apple's on-device agent and generative features framework. It stores and manages agent session state locally so a task or conversation can survive between launches; it is a recent addition to macOS."),
        ("bare-modifier-monitor", "A small helper bundled inside ChatGPT.app. It watches for bare modifier key presses, such as a double-tap of Option, so the app's global shortcut can summon its window from anywhere without claiming a normal hotkey."),
        ("ciphermld", "A daemon supporting Private Information Retrieval and Private Encrypted Compute. These let your Mac look something up on Apple's servers without the server learning what was asked, so features that need a lookup do not have to reveal your query."),
        ("contentlinkingd", "A per-user agent in Apple's Synapse framework. It builds links between related pieces of content on your Mac so Spotlight and Siri Suggestions can connect, for example, a message to the contact or event it refers to."),
        ("dmd", "The device management daemon behind Apple's DeviceManagement framework. It applies declarative configuration pushed by an MDM server; on a personal Mac with no management profile installed it simply idles."),
        ("heard", "A launchd agent in the accessibility subsystem. It backs macOS hearing features, including support for hearing devices and audio routing to them, and does real work only when those features are turned on."),
        ("icdd", "The Image Capture discovery daemon. It watches for connected scanners and cameras and matches each one to the right driver module, which is what makes a device appear in Image Capture, Preview, or a scan dialog."),
        ("logind", "The login session daemon. It creates and tears down the per-user session that everything you run lives inside, handling login and logout, fast user switching, and the transitions around the login window."),
        ("lskdd", "A daemon in Apple's FairPlay content protection stack. It obtains and manages the decryption keys for DRM-protected video, so it turns up when you play something from Apple TV or another protected streaming service."),
        ("mbuseragent", "A helper shipped inside Setup Assistant. It supports the first-run setup and Migration Assistant flows, handling the transfer side of moving your files, apps and settings over from another Mac."),
        ("milod", "A per-user daemon providing MicroLocation services. It works out precisely where a nearby device is rather than just that it is in range, which is the technology behind direction-aware device finding and proximity-triggered features."),
        ("mobilerepaird", "A daemon supporting Apple's repair and service flows. It checks the identity and calibration status of serviceable parts such as a battery or display, and it is what surfaces the notice that a part could not be verified as genuine."),
        ("mobiletimerd", "The timer agent, shared with iOS. It tracks alarms, timers and sleep schedules and is responsible for posting the notification and playing the sound when one fires, whether or not the Clock app is open."),
        ("ospredictiond", "A daemon that predicts upcoming system conditions, such as when the Mac will be idle, plugged in, or on a good network. Schedulers use those predictions to run background work at a moment that will not cost you battery or responsiveness."),
        ("replicatord", "A daemon that syncs data for remote widgets. It pulls the content behind widgets that come from your other Apple devices, and it also backs the remote device data used by iPhone Mirroring."),
        ("reversetemplated", "A daemon that works alongside suggestd to find structured information in your content. It scans text handed to it for things like dates, addresses and flight numbers, which is what produces the Found in Apps suggestions you see in Calendar and Contacts."),
        ("searchtoold", "A per-user agent in the OmniSearch framework. It backs the suggestion side of system search, supplying the results and completions that appear as you type into Spotlight and similar search fields."),
    ]
}
